The transcript-backed rhythm is concrete: $\begin{aligned}
t_{\mathrm{wake}} &= 4{:}30\ \mathrm{a.m.},\\
t_{\mathrm{gym}}  &= 5{:}00\ \mathrm{a.m.}.\end{aligned}$ The frequency is also concrete, but still personal rather than prescriptive: $\begin{aligned}
f_{\mathrm{workout}} &\approx 7\ \text{days/week},\\
f_{\mathrm{workout}} &\approx 6\ \text{days/week}\quad \text{on some weeks},\\
n_{\mathrm{workouts/day}} &= 2\quad \text{on some days}.\end{aligned}$ The book should not turn those numbers into doctrine. Their value is evidential. This speaker has made the gym part of the operating rhythm before the office has a chance to spend the whole day.

