p.src:
The closing image is Abraham Lincoln sharpening the axe. The speaker recalls the saying as four hours to chop down a tree, with the first three spent sharpening: $\begin{aligned}
T_{\mathrm{total}}   &= 4\ \text{hours},\\
T_{\mathrm{sharpen}} &= 3\ \text{hours},\\
T_{\mathrm{chop}}    &= 1\ \text{hour}.\end{aligned}$ So the anecdotal preparation share is $\frac{T_{\mathrm{sharpen}}}{T_{\mathrm{total}}}
=
\frac{3}{4}.$ This is not a literal schedule recommendation. Its use in the book is narrower: preparation and recovery are part of the work because they maintain the tool.

